---
import Callout from "astro-theme-university/components/Callout.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import SessionsGrid from "../../components/SessionsGrid.astro";
import heroImage from "../../assets/images/sessions-hero.avif";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../../site-config";

// Raw anchors in .astro are emitted verbatim — unlike markdown links, nothing
// rewrites them for the Pages sub-path, and the theme's base check fails the
// build. Trailing slash trimmed so the join works whichever form BASE_URL takes.
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<ContentLayout
  title={sessionLabels.plural}
  description="Twelve tutorials, and the twelve things you have to bring to them. This is where the course actually happens."
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A fieldwork table seen at an angle: an open handwritten notebook, a kitchen scale, a mason jar mid-ferment, a tape measure, two magnifying glasses, a pencil, index cards and a screwdriver with a ruler, arranged across a teal tabletop on a warm cream ground."
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <p>
    The lectures make the argument. The sessions test it against evidence from a
    real kitchen — usually yours. Almost every week asks you to arrive with
    something you gathered beforehand: photographs, a drawing, a week of
    records, ten date labels, a clean jar. The seminar runs on what the room
    found, not on what the room read, and a session where half the group arrived
    empty is a different and much worse class.
  </p>

  <p>
    Sessions run <strong>Wednesdays 14:00–16:00</strong> in the Cold Storage Building
    (7), Room 1.03. Week 11 meets in the teaching kitchen instead — it is a fermentation
    workshop, and you will need a sink.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each page below states that week's question, what to prepare, how the two
    hours are spent, and what leaves the room. It also names the claim it
    inherits from the week before, so the twelve weeks read as one argument
    rather than twelve topics that happen to share a subject.
  </p>

  <Callout type="warning">
    <p>
      <strong>Two exercises cannot be made up later.</strong> The
      <a href={`${base}/sessions/01-artificial-cold/`}>week 1 blind tasting</a> cannot
      be repeated once you know what the samples were, and the
      <a href={`${base}/sessions/08-systemic-waste/`}>week 8 waste audit</a> depends
      on records kept at the time. If you will miss either, tell us in advance and
      we will arrange an alternative rather than write it off.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <SessionsGrid />
</ContentLayout>

{
  /*
  Same treatment as the Lectures hero and the people detail pages: this
  illustration is a vignette on its own cream, not a full-bleed scene, so the
  theme's default cover-and-scrim (built for art that survives a hard crop)
  would enlarge and cut it. object-fit: contain, right-aligned, dark title on
  the art's own cream — sampled at #f9f4de, not the theme's --at-bg-alt token,
  for the same near-miss-seam reason recorded on those other pages.

  is:global reaches .at-hero because it belongs to the theme's Hero markup,
  outside this component's own tree; scoped by construction to this file, so
  the home page's dark full-bleed hero is untouched.

  `.at-hero-title` is pinned to a fixed dark ink in light mode rather than
  `var(--at-text)`, since that token is light-dark()-aware and this cream
  band doesn't move — flipped explicitly to white under [data-theme="dark"]
  alongside the band itself, which goes to --at-black. The image is dimmed
  in dark mode so its baked cream doesn't sit as a bright box on black.
*/
}
<style is:global>
  .at-hero {
    background: #f7f1db;
  }
  .at-hero-image {
    object-fit: contain;
    object-position: right center;
    outline: none;
  }
  .at-hero-content {
    background: none;
  }
  .at-hero-title {
    color: oklch(from var(--at-primary) 20% 0.01 h);
    text-shadow: none;
  }
  [data-theme="dark"] .at-hero {
    background: var(--at-black);
  }
  [data-theme="dark"] .at-hero-title {
    color: var(--at-white);
  }
  [data-theme="dark"] .at-hero-image {
    filter: brightness(0.82) contrast(0.94);
  }
  @media (width < 48rem) {
    .at-hero-image {
      object-position: center top;
      opacity: 0.2;
    }
  }
</style>
